<h2>Two versions of one departure</h2>
<p>The FIR narrated an armed abduction: men arriving in a white vehicle, a minor girl taken at gunpoint, a recovery from Jaipur months later. The bail record told a different story. In her statement under Section 183 BNSS, the prosecutrix said she had left of her own volition and married the petitioner; she was about three months pregnant. A Medical Board assessed her age at between 18 and 20 years. The petitioner had been in custody since 23 March 2026 and had no criminal antecedents.</p>

<h2>What the Court ordered</h2>
<p>Weighing the statement and the age assessment, the Court directed release: <em>&ldquo;let the petitioner, above named be released on bail on furnishing bail bond of Rs.10,000/-&rdquo;</em> with two sureties of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the District &amp; Additional Sessions Judge-cum-Special Judge, POCSO Act, Supaul. The order pairs liberty with verification — the trial court is to check the petitioner&rsquo;s antecedents, and concealment can trigger cancellation proceedings — while protecting the release itself: acceptance of the bail bond <em>&ldquo;shall not be delayed for purpose of or in the name of verification.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>Age and consent at the bail stage</h2>
<p>POCSO prosecutions arising from elopements present a recurring structure: a complaint by the family framed as kidnapping, a victim&rsquo;s later statement describing a voluntary relationship, and contested age evidence. Courts navigate them with two settled propositions. First, consent is legally irrelevant if the prosecutrix was a child — so age is the fulcrum. Second, at the bail stage the court takes only a prima facie view: a medical band straddling the age of majority, combined with the victim&rsquo;s own account, can justify release without deciding the age question, which the trial court must resolve on the statutory hierarchy of proof drawn from Section 94 of the Juvenile Justice Act — matriculation or school records first, birth certificate next, medical opinion last.</p>
